Keep the counter boot storage out of the recyclable segment

SCALABLE_COUNTER_DEFINE puts __early_storage in __startup_data, which on arm64
resolves to __BOOTDATA,__init.  __BOOTDATA is boot-only memory: pmap hands it
back to kmem once the kernel's own allocator is up, and it is taken from the end
of the segment first.

scalable_counter_static_init reads that storage during PERCPU, which runs after
KMEM.  run-236 puts the third counter's storage at 0x7bac008 -- inside the last
page of __init, whose section ends at 0x7bac640 -- and the read there never
returns, while the counters stored in earlier pages read fine.  That is the
signature of a page that has already been handed out.

Pin the counter's boot storage to __DATA instead.  It costs eight bytes per
counter for the life of the kernel and removes the race entirely.
---
diff --git a/osfmk/kern/counter.h b/osfmk/kern/counter.h
--- a/osfmk/kern/counter.h
+++ b/osfmk/kern/counter.h
@@ -130,5 +130,6 @@
 #define SCALABLE_COUNTER_DEFINE(name) \
-	__startup_data uint64_t __ ##name##_early_storage = 0;                                   \
+	/* nio: __BOOTDATA is recycled by kmem before PERCPU reads it */                          \
+	__attribute__((section("__DATA,__data"))) uint64_t __ ##name##_early_storage = 0;         \
 	scalable_counter_t name = {&__##name##_early_storage};                                   \
 	STARTUP_ARG(TUNABLES, STARTUP_RANK_MIDDLE, scalable_counter_static_boot_mangle, &name);  \
 	STARTUP_ARG(PERCPU, STARTUP_RANK_SECOND, scalable_counter_static_init, &name);

